WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.030
Man, monster, myth — Count Dracula is all
of these things and more.

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:08.860
But who is the man behind the fangs in The
Last Voyage of the Demeter?

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:12.610
There have been a lot of live-action versions
of Dracula over the years, but few of them

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:17.710
are as outright monstrous as the creature
featured in 2023's The Last Voyage of the Demeter.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:22.560
This version of the classic vampire is an
absolute horror to behold, with bat-like wings,

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:26.230
elongated ears, and a mouth full of razor
sharp teeth.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:30.090
"Satan's black blood pumping corruption through
his veins.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:32.739
God's anger has come upon this vessel like
Jonah!"

00:00:32.739 --> 00:00:33.902
"Shut up!"

00:00:34.656 --> 00:00:38.340
It's such a ghoulish, inhuman rendition of
the character, in fact, that it may be easy

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:42.230
for audiences to forget that there's an actor
under all that prosthetic makeup.

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:46.540
The Dracula featured in The Last Voyage of
the Demeter is played by none other than Javier

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:51.180
Botet, and the drastic difference in appearance
between the real-life actor and his on-screen

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.750
persona may prove shocking to some viewers.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:58.200
If Botet's name sounds familiar, it's probably
because the performer has been routinely involved

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:03.842
in major horror films for well over 15 years
— and in most cases, he's playing something spooky.

00:01:03.842 --> 00:01:08.250
For the bulk of his career in film and television,
Javier Botet has worked as a specialized type

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.070
of actor known as a creature performer.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:14.830
As the name implies, that means he more often
than not portrays inhuman or monstrous characters

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:18.510
that require a lot of preparation by way of
prosthetics and makeup.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:22.950
In addition to Dracula, Botet has played some
of the most terrifying horror movie villains out there.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:27.350
He appeared as the Crooked Man in The Conjuring
2, a Xenomorph in Alien: Covenant, various

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:32.784
forms of It in It and It Chapter Two, and
Slender Man in Slender Man — to name but a few.

00:01:32.784 --> 00:01:35.210
Suffice to say, he knows his way around horror.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:38.860
Interestingly enough, Botet has the unique
capacity to physically manipulate his body

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:41.770
— something which he uses to great effect
in his roles.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:43.600
In an interview with the BBC, he said:

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.579
"I have a disease called Marfan syndrome.

00:01:45.579 --> 00:01:48.730
It makes people skinnier, taller, and very
flexible.

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:53.219
So I was all my life very flexible, all my
life doing weird tricks and things to enjoy

00:01:53.219 --> 00:01:55.610
with my friends [using] very creepy movement."

00:01:55.610 --> 00:02:00.610
While many of Botet's biggest roles are scare-filled
creature flicks, he has also amassed credits

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:04.479
in non-horror productions — some of which
have him in prosthetics, while others don't.

00:02:04.479 --> 00:02:09.220
Recent years have also seen him cross over
to mainstream sci-fi and fantasy franchises,

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:13.910
appearing as an undead wight in Game of Thrones
and one of the alien Ba'ul in Star Trek Discovery.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:18.470
While Botet has played a cavalcade of mainstay
monsters in his time, he had long awaited

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:21.410
the opportunity to sink his teeth into the
role of Dracula.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:25.670
The actor did once play the character all
the way back in a 2007 episode of the Spanish

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:29.890
series Los hombres de Paco, but he wanted
nothing more than to bring the horror icon

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:31.980
to life in a full-fledged monster movie.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.310
Last Voyage of the Demeter turned out to be
his big shot.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:39.519
In an interview with Screen Rant, Botet spoke
about getting cast as Dracula, as well as

00:02:39.519 --> 00:02:42.870
reuniting with director André Øvredal on
Last Voyage of the Demeter.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:43.870
He said:

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:46.580
"For me, it would be an honor, and finally
it happened.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:50.580
It was great to know that André Øvredal
was directing, because after a lot of projects,

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:54.220
you need to meet a lot of new people, it's
fine, but always when you are going to need

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:57.450
a big crew, when you've worked already with
somebody, it's easier, it helps.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.920
I remember it was so easy, and so nice."

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.519
Botet particularly relished the chance to
portray Øvredal's unique take on Dracula

00:03:04.519 --> 00:03:09.080
— a brutal, animalistic being who transforms
from a weakened wretch to a terrifyingly powerful

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:10.280
force of darkness.

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:11.280
As he says:

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.170
"Our Dracula was not a classical, sexy guy,
so for me, it was good to try something different,

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:17.040
something more heavy."

